blocks.text:
&ldquo;And this is Tuesday&mdash;I don't know how I'm ever going to wait, ma!
      The time does drag along so, and I'm so dying to see them! Which of them
      do you reckon is the tallest, ma?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, it's well enough. I'd like both of them better if I knew the way to
      pronounce them&mdash;the Eyetalian way, I mean. The Missouri way and the
      Eyetalian way is different, I judge.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, there's never any telling. These foreigners with that kind of names
      are so delicate, and of course that kind of names are not suited to our
      climate&mdash;you wouldn't expect it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Always been used to sleeping together&mdash;in-fact, prefer it. And I was
      thinking it was to accommodate me. I thought it was very good of them,
      whereas a person situated as that young man is&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;I'm not so certain about that. The one on the left&mdash;I mean the one
      on it's left&mdash;hasn't near as good a face, in my opinion, as its
      brother.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, Angelo, I reckon, though I can't tell t'other from which by their
      names, yet awhile. But it's the right-hand one&mdash;the blond one. He has
      such kind blue eyes, and curly copper hair and fresh complexion&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;Poor thing, I hope it won't catch cold; it was powerful wet, just
      drenched, you may say. I hope it has left its boots outside, so they can
      be dried.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Sho, of course; I never thought of that. Well, come along, maybe we can
      get some sleep, but I don't know, I'm so shook up with what we've been
      through.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Talk as if what? I know one thing mighty certain: if you can fix me so I
      can eat for two and only have to stub toes for one, I ain't going to fool
      away no such chance just for sentiment.&rdquo;

&ldquo;My friend, when I am in command of our body, I choose my apparel
      according to my own convenience, as I have remarked more than several
      times already. When you are in command, I beg you will do as you please.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Luigi, if you would only smoke a little milder tobacco, I am sure I could
      learn not to mind it in time, but this is so strong, and the pipe is so
      rank that&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;Ah, brother, that is a strong word&mdash;everlasting&mdash;and isn't
      quite fair. I only complain when I suffocate; you know I don't complain
      when we are in the open air.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Mercy on me, how should I know, and I've hardly set eyes on them yet.&rdquo;
      After a pause she added, &ldquo;They made considerable noise after they went
      up.&rdquo;

&ldquo;But, ma, I think it must be a foreign custom; and it must be right too;
      and the best way, because they have had every opportunity to know what is
      right, and it don't stand to reason that with their education they would
      do anything but what the highest musical authorities have sanctioned. You
      can't help but admit that, ma.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh no, madam, I am very much obliged, but I never drink coffee, much as I
      would like to. You did see me take up Luigi's cup, it is true, but if you
      noticed, I didn't carry it to my mouth, but to his.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, that depends&mdash;that depends. I knew a man who fell down a well
      twice. He said he didn't mind the first time, but he thought the second
      time was once too often. Have I misused that word, Mrs. Cooper?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, shut up, can't you!&rdquo; said the widow, embarrassed and irritated. &ldquo;Give
      me all your hands, I want to shake them all; for I know you are both just
      as good as you can be.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Ah, Luigi is always the fortunate one when honors are showering,&rdquo; said
      Angelo, plaintively; &ldquo;now what have I done, Mrs. Cooper, that you leave me
      out? Come, you must strain a point in my favor.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Don't you breathe a syllable to a soul&mdash;I'm going to tell you
      something. In my opinion Tom Driscoll's chances were considerable better
      yesterday than they are to-day.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, I can't tell about that yet. But we'll see. If I find you deserve
      it I'll take you under my protection and make your fame and fortune for
      you.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Your honor, the question is an irrelevant triviality. Necessarily, they
      both kicked him, for they have but the one pair of legs, and both are
      responsible for them.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, in the first place, the insult was given to both of them equally,
      for they were called a pair of scissors. Of course they would both want to
      resent it, and so&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, sir, but of course that was another mistake, and not intentional. He
      wouldn't drink it. He set it down.&rdquo; A slight pause, then he added,
      casually and quite simply: &ldquo;The plaintiff reached for it and hogged it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, I&mdash;I'm sure I don't quite know what to think, but I reckon
      that one of them did half of the kick and the other one did the other
      half.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Wait a moment, gentlemen,&rdquo; he said, &ldquo;it is plain that an assault has been
      committed it is plain to anybody; but the way things are going, the guilty
      will certainly escape conviction. I can not allow this. Now&mdash;-&rdquo;

&ldquo;No wrong will be done your clients, sir&mdash;they will be protected;
      also the public and the offended laws. Mr. Allen, you will amend your
      pleadings, and put one of the accused on trial at a time.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Never mind about that, Mr. Allen. It may not all be hearsay. We shall
      see. It may at least help to put us on the right track. Call the
      witnesses.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Now we are getting at the kernel of the thing,&rdquo; observed the judge, with
      grave and earnest satisfaction. &ldquo;It looks as if the Count Luigi was in
      possession of the battery at the time of the assault.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, we walked; and as it was from the extreme edge of the town, and
      there was no hurry, I judge it took us about twenty minutes, maybe a
      trifle more.&rdquo;

&ldquo;It would be well if others were as thoughtful. Was anything said, between
      the conversation at my house and the assault, upon the detail which we are
      now examining into?&rdquo;

&ldquo;If power over the mutual legs was in the possession of one brother at
      nine, and passed into the possession of the other one during the next
      thirty or forty minutes, do you think you could have detected the change?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Hear it? I should think so,&rdquo; said Aunt Betsy, rising and facing the
      court. &ldquo;Why, Judge, I was there and heard it myself. Luigi says to Angelo&mdash;no,
      it was Angelo said it to&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, it does seem to me that you are most cantankerously particular
      about a little thing, Sim Robinson. Why, when I can't remember a thing
      myself, I always&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;Now how can she when you keep fussing at her all the time?&rdquo; said Aunt
      Betsy. &ldquo;Why, with a person pecking at me that way, I should get that
      fuzzled and fuddled that&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;Madam, do you know&mdash;do you absolutely know, independently of
      anything these gentlemen have told you&mdash;that the power over their
      legs passes from the one to the other regularly every week?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Regularly? Bless your heart, regularly ain't any name for the exactness
      of it! All the big cities in Europe used to set the clocks by it.&rdquo;
      (Laughter, suppressed by the court.)

&ldquo;And so would I, Patsy Cooper. It seems to me that if we can save these
      poor persecuted strangers, it is our bounden duty to stand up here and
      talk for them till&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;What in the world are you doing? Straighten up! What's the matter with
      you?&mdash;you're in no danger&mdash;nobody's going to shoot at you.
      Straighten up, I tell you!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Count Luigi's request for another exchange is another proof that he is a
      brave and chivalrous gentleman, and I beg that the courtesy he asks may be
      accorded him.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, certainly&mdash;at present. When the violent and continuous
      perspiring has reduced your strength, I shall have to reduce your diet, of
      course, and also bleed you, but there is no occasion for that yet awhile.&rdquo;
      He turned to Aunt Patsy and said: &ldquo;He must be put to bed, and sat up with,
      and tended with the greatest care, and not allowed to stir for several
      days and nights.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I will cheerfully submit to all your requirements, sir, up to two o'clock
      this afternoon, and will resume them after three, but cannot be confined
      to the house during that intermediate hour.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, don't fret, it wasn't in any danger,&rdquo; said Luigi, irritably; &ldquo;they
      wouldn't waste it for a little thing like that; there's a glass case all
      ready for it in the heavenly museum, and a pin to stick it up with.&rdquo;

&ldquo;&mdash;and a shovel of fire for his head, and a mustard plaster for his
      neck, and some gum shoes for his ears,&rdquo; Luigi interrupted, with temper;
      and added, to himself, &ldquo;Damnation, I'm going to be roasted alive, I just
      know it!&rdquo;

&ldquo;I don't&mdash;to that extent, Aunt Patsy. I was glad the drowning was
      postponed a minute ago, but I'm not now. No, that is all gone by; I want
      to be drowned.&rdquo;

&ldquo;You'll bring a judgment on yourself just as sure as you live, if you go
      on like that. Why, I never heard the beat of it. Now, there&mdash;there!
      you've said enough. Not another word out of you&mdash;I won't have it!&rdquo;

&ldquo;No, I believe not, Aunt Patsy. If I don't get in earlier, I'll be along
      by early candle-light, anyway. Meantime, don't allow him to get out of his
      bed.&rdquo;

The boy Henry was troubled, but the boy Joe said, inaudibly, and
      gratefully, &ldquo;We're all honky, after all; and no postponement on account of
      the weather.&rdquo;

&ldquo;'Sh-h-h! Will you be quiet, Luigi! Here, now, take the rest of it&mdash;don't
      keep me holding the dipper all night. I declare if you haven't left a good
      fourth of it in the bottom! Come&mdash;that's a good&mdash;

&ldquo;Aunt Betsy, don't make me! I feel like I've swallowed a cemetery; I do,
      indeed. Do let me rest a little&mdash;just a little; I can't take any more
      of the devilish stuff now.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Because I've caught a cold in my head. I always do, when I go in the
      water with my clothes on. And it takes me weeks to get over it, too. I
      think it was a shame to serve me so.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Luigi, you are unreasonable; you know very well they couldn't baptize him
      dry. I should think you would be willing to undergo a little inconvenience
      for your brother's sake.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Pudd'nhead was right at the start&mdash;we ought to have hired the
      official half of that human phillipene to resign; but it's too late now;
      some of us haven't got anything left to hire him with.&rdquo;
